      en: "The El Naranjo ruins are an archaeological Maya site in the Copán department of Honduras, near the border with Guatemala. The site is located in the valley of the Copán River and was inhabited between 600 and 900 AD. It includes several pyramids, a ball court, and residential structures. The buildings are made of local limestone. The site was discovered in 1935 and excavated in 1956. It is smaller than the nearby Copán site, but significant for understanding Maya civilization in the region. The ruins are surrounded by tropical forest. Access is restricted, and the site is managed by the Honduran Institute of Anthropology and History.",
